# evidence-for: FR-020, AC-025, NFR-002
# evidence-volatile: \d+\.\d+s
# evidence-volatile: Installed \d+ packages in \d+ms
# evidence-timeout: 300
#
# D-128 -- D-097's fix was applied to the SINGLE door and not the BATCH door,
# so a filed defect was still silently discarded.
#
# `foundry_sync_defects` ran `[d for d in records if d.get("status") ==
# "fixed"]` INSIDE the ledger transaction: a raw .get() over every historical
# record, no isinstance guard. foundry.py already carried the remedy --
# `_dict_records`, added BY D-097, whose docstring says "Tolerating it in ONE
# place is what keeps the two halves of the same scan consistent" -- and the
# batch door did not use it. Two more raw scans sat in the same block: the
# reopen pass matched on `d["id"]`, and the closing count re-scanned the list.
#
# WHY IT LOSES DATA rather than merely erroring. The raise lands AFTER the
# in-transaction list is mutated, so `_save_json` never runs, the transaction
# aborts, and the filing the stream just made is GONE. The caller receives a
# traceback that names no file. That is the one row breaking D-095/D-098's
# stated bar of 21/24 named refusals: shape-8 x Sync raised AND lost, while
# Foundry-Defect persisted the record and preserved the malformed one, and
# Foundry-Defects read clean.
#
# THE PARITY FRAME. Asserted across BOTH doors rather than as a Sync outcome,
# for the reason the cycle-stamp rows in this suite already use: two filing
# doors that disagree about what a defect ledger may contain IS the defect.
# Three properties, six non-dict shapes, both doors: neither raises, neither
# loses the filing it was handed, and neither DROPS the junk record --
# tolerating a malformed record must not mean deleting it, which would be a
# quieter D-096 (refusing to lose records to a bad container while losing them
# to a bad record).
#
# BOTH SIDES OF THE CLASS ARE NOW CLOSED, and the second arrived mid-cycle.
# Casting 3's D-127 moved the filter INSIDE `ledger_transaction`, which now
# yields mapping records only and re-inserts the non-dicts by index before the
# write. So the orchestrator's `_dict_records` calls are idempotent today
# rather than load-bearing. They are kept deliberately: the package scan in
# test_orchestrator_gates.py asserts the orchestrator reaches records through
# the filter rather than resting on what a sibling module currently does
# inside a contextmanager, so a revert there, a second ledger primitive, or a
# caller binding the raw document surfaces as a named offender instead of as a
# silently reopened D-128.
